    q: "What is the bias-variance tradeoff?",
    level: 'Basics',
    a: "Bias is error from a model that is too simple and misses the pattern (underfitting). Variance is error from a model that is too sensitive to the training data and changes a lot with new samples (overfitting). Making a model more complex lowers bias but raises variance, so I look for the sweet spot, usually by checking validation scores across model complexity. Here degree 1 underfits (high bias) and a very high degree overfits (high variance).",
    code: "import numpy as np\n\nrng = np.random.RandomState(0)\nx = rng.rand(40) * 6\ny = np.sin(x) + rng.randn(40) * 0.2\nx_tr, y_tr, x_te, y_te = x[:30], y[:30], x[30:], y[30:]\n\nfor degree in [1, 4, 12]:\n    coefs = np.polyfit(x_tr, y_tr, degree)\n    error = np.mean((np.polyval(coefs, x_te) - y_te) ** 2)\n    print(f\"degree={degree}: test error={error:.3f}\")",
