    q: "What are transformers and the attention mechanism?",
    level: 'Advanced',
    a: "A transformer is the architecture behind modern LLMs. Its key idea is self-attention: each token builds a query, key and value, scores how relevant every other token is (query dot key, scaled and softmaxed), and takes a weighted mix of their values. This lets the model look at the whole sequence at once and run in parallel, unlike RNNs.",
    code: "import numpy as np\n\nnp.random.seed(0)\nX = np.random.randn(3, 4)                  # 3 tokens, 4 numbers each\nWq, Wk, Wv = np.random.randn(3, 4, 4)\n\nQ, K, V = X @ Wq, X @ Wk, X @ Wv\nscores = Q @ K.T / np.sqrt(4)              # query . key, scaled\nweights = np.exp(scores) / np.exp(scores).sum(axis=1, keepdims=True)   # softmax\nprint(weights.round(2))                    # each row sums to 1\nprint((weights @ V).shape)                 # weighted mix of values",
